{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both configs are repaired and the diagnostic wrapper is gone. Pushed as `e695d5b`.\n\n## The root cause\n\nYour session log showed `!!! caught SIGTERM` in the same second the hook started. Here's why:\n\n**Claude Code reads a hook's `timeout` as seconds. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code copied Claude's hook config format but not its unit** — both pass the number straight to `setTimeout`. Their own code says so: `Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`, `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`.\n\n`cledger install` wrote `timeout: 120` into all three. On Claude Code that's 120 seconds. On Gemini and Qwen it's **120 milliseconds**. A cold capture is ~85ms of work on top of Node's startup, so the hook lost that race nearly every time — and whether `appendEvents` finished before the signal landed decided whether your events survived. That's the whole mystery: constant \"failed\" warnings, yet events sometimes appearing anyway.\n\nI also found the PR's explanation of `>/dev/null 2>&1` is simply wrong. It claimed the redirect makes the work \"a grandchild that outlives the teardown.\" Both CLIs *always* invoke hooks as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` **execs** a single simple command rather than forking — nothing was ever detached. All the redirect did was hide the error, which is exactly why this took so long to find. I've corrected the comment and left the suffix in place, since removing it is a separate untested change.\n\n`install` now also **repairs** a wrong timeout in place. Previously it saw \"a cledger hook already exists\" and returned — so nobody already installed could have fixed this by re-running it.\n\n## Test 2 passes\n\nWith the timeout fixed:\n\n```\nPASS  2 rewind activity/activities recorded\n      seq=9 withdrew 3 message(s)\nPASS  every withdrawn message is still captured as a turn (union capture holds)\n      seq=15 withdrew 6 message(s)\nPASS  every withdrawn message is still captured as a turn (union capture holds)\n```\n\nOne correction to my earlier work: the first FAIL you'd have seen was **my checker's bug**, not the adapter's — it read `cledger log --json`, which drops `raw`. Only `export` is lossless. Fixed.\n\n## And the thing that actually justifies the project\n\nRemember `c0ebebbe`, the session whose file vanished? It's in the ledger:\n\n```\nseq=3  human  what is 2+2?\nseq=5  agent  Okay, I've processed the query...\nseq=9  human  what is 10+10?\nseq=11 agent  10 + 10 = 20.\n```\n\n**Gemini deleted its own session file after the rewind. The ledger's copy is the only surviving record of that conversation.** That's not a contrived demo — it happened to you, by accident, during a smoke test. I've noted it in the README.\n\n## What's left\n\nTest 3 needs redoing — it was invalid before, since the hook never captured the killed session, and the sweep correctly ignores sessions it has never seen. Now that hooks actually work, it's a real test:\n\n**Test 3 (redo)** — exactly as before: start `gemini`, ask something, **let the answer finish**, `kill -9` it from another terminal, run `node ~/tmp/cledger-check.mjs 3a`, start a second session, ask anything, `/quit`, then `node ~/tmp/cledger-check.mjs 3b`.\n\n**Tests 4 and 5 (Qwen)** — unchanged from my earlier instructions, and now worth more: Qwen had the same 120ms bug, so its hooks were also being killed. Use the local endpoint:\n```bash\ncd ~/tmp/cledger-smoke\nexport OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001/v1\nexport OPENAI_API_KEY=dummy\nexport OPENAI_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash\nqwen\n```\nthen `summarize @calc.py`, `/chat save smoke-test`, `/agents`, `/quit`, then `node ~/tmp/cledger-check.mjs 4`.\n\nYou should see **no more \"hook failed\" warnings** in either CLI. If you do, tell me — that would mean something beyond the timeout.\n\nresult:\nRoot-caused the hook failures: Gemini CLI and Qwen Code read a hook's `timeout` as milliseconds while Claude Code reads seconds, so cledger's `timeout: 120` 